::MCR_U06_L03_GuidedPractice_Q01::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L03_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.4; MLA.MCR.GM.5; MLA.MCR.GM.6
Question:
A rectangle is 9 feet long and 4 feet wide. What is its area?
{
~A. 13 square feet#This adds length and width. Area of a rectangle uses length times width.
=B. 36 square feet#Correct. Area \= 9 x 4 \= 36 square feet.
~C. 36 cubic feet#The number is correct, but area uses square units, not cubic units.
~D. 18 square feet#This doubles one dimension instead of multiplying length by width.
}
::MCR_U06_L03_GuidedPractice_Q02::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L03_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.4; MLA.MCR.GM.5; MLA.MCR.GM.6
Question:
A rectangular prism is 5 inches long, 3 inches wide, and 2 inches high. What is its volume?
{
~A. 10 cubic inches#This multiplies only two dimensions. Volume needs length, width, and height.
~B. 30 square inches#The number is correct, but volume uses cubic units, not square units.
~C. 25 cubic inches#This does not multiply all three dimensions correctly.
=D. 30 cubic inches#Correct. Volume \= 5 x 3 x 2 \= 30 cubic inches.
}
::MCR_U06_L03_GuidedPractice_Q03::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L03_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.4; MLA.MCR.GM.5; MLA.MCR.GM.6
Question:
Which situation asks for surface area?
{
=A. Finding how much paper is needed to wrap a box#Correct. Wrapping covers the outside faces, so this asks for surface area.
~B. Finding how much water fills a tank#Filling asks for volume because it measures inside space.
~C. Finding the floor space of a room#Floor space is flat area, not total outside surface area.
~D. Finding the length of a hallway#Length is one-dimensional, not surface area.
}
::MCR_U06_L03_GuidedPractice_Q04::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L03_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.4; MLA.MCR.GM.5; MLA.MCR.GM.6
Question:
A cube has side length 3 cm. What is its volume?
{
~A. 9 cubic cm#This finds the area of one face, not the volume.
~B. 18 cubic cm#This finds 6 x 3, not side x side x side.
=C. 27 cubic cm#Correct. Volume of a cube \= 3 x 3 x 3 \= 27 cubic cm.
~D. 27 square cm#The number is correct, but volume uses cubic units.
}
::MCR_U06_L03_GuidedPractice_Q05::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L03_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.4; MLA.MCR.GM.5; MLA.MCR.GM.6
Question:
Which unit should label the area of a wall?
{
~A. cubic feet#Cubic feet measure volume. A wall is a flat surface, so area uses square units.
=B. square feet#Correct. Area measures flat space and uses square units.
~C. feet#Feet measure length only. Area needs square units.
~D. gallons#Gallons measure volume or capacity, not wall area.
}
